.5dslNzzdaly" displaytext="Selecting Texts for a Close Reading" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.63xAJGAN6uI.6DqbsMDzbkd" displaytext="Structuring Questions to Guide a Close Reading" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.63xAJGAN6uI.5uxz67BTFb9" displaytext="Structuring Reflection of a Close Reading" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.63xAJGAN6uI.64IY13jT1P6" displaytext="Close Reading across Disciplines" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.63xAJGAN6uI.6nXtKrbCg5T" displaytext="Close Reading Quick-Check" expand="true" /></links></slidelink><slidelink slideid="_player.6VnXrL2qblR.6fW2JUgwvPo" displaytext="In Practice" expand="false"><links><slidelink slideid="_player.6VnXrL2qblR.5yr2QAOZyRy" displaytext="Video: Close Reading in Action" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6VnXrL2qblR.6WteRdaFnjY" displaytext="Close Reading in Action Reflection" expand="true" /></links></slidelink><slidelink slideid="_player.5kUV0fkYWnz.6O52XnadHjJ" displaytext="Apply" expand="false"><links><slidelink slideid="_player.5kUV0fkYWnz.5u5GEH7L9O9" displaytext="Implementing Close Reading into Your Instruction" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.5kUV0fkYWnz.5V2XzZJvxnx" displaytext="Selecting a Short, Worthy Text " expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.5kUV0fkYWnz.6WlQtR2c3L4" displaytext="Planning Instruction: What will I model for students?" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.5kUV0fkYWnz.5iG741UfLSp" displaytext="How will I guide students through practice?" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.5kUV0fkYWnz.6C9ra2TDnGr" displaytext="What will students do independently?" expand="true" /></links></slidelink><slidelink slideid="_player.6bMBZx0L5ld.6anlHbEJ1hA" displaytext="Reflect" expand="false"><links><slidelink slideid="_player.6bMBZx0L5ld.5cN1yA07wjF" displaytext="Reflection of Learning" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6bMBZx0L5ld.5XHj5UeHUpW" displaytext="Review Course Outcomes" expand="true" /></links></slidelink><slidelink slideid="_player.6QuSGxA16uD.65SEJjbLsuw" displaytext="Look Forward" expand="false"><links><slidelink slideid="_player.6QuSGxA16uD.6UjQg7tzjGu" displaytext="Assignments" expand="true" /><slidelink slideid="_player.6QuSGxA16uD.5tboUVrPLri" displaytext="In Preparation for the Next Session" expand="true" /></links></slidelink></links></outline><search><slidetext slideid="5XSzB7jZajQ.6MlS3J7yuGh" slidebank="false">vocabulary their way self-paced session 3: building vocabulary through close reading of complex text tm </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6IjT3uCZGfL.60CRu1RJTZk" slidebank="false">welcome </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6IjT3uCZGfL.5jALJGZVNFv" slidebank="false">by the end of this blended course, you  will be able to do the following: understand vocabulary development and spelling-meaning connections. analyze vocabulary assessments to identify students’ needs and organize for instruction.  determine how best to instruct students using generative and word-specific strategies. select appropriate instructional strategies and activities to expand students’ academic vocabulary.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6IjT3uCZGfL.5wve6KIHWRu" slidebank="false">in your participant workbook, each of the close reading lesson suggestions are listed. choose the two suggestions that resonated with you the most and write a short reflection about why you feel the suggestion is important for effective close reading: design the lesson so students reread. ask students to read with a pencil. model the text. remind students to note confusion. discuss the text. ask text-dependent questions.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="63xAJGAN6uI.6ahnMkhpOck" slidebank="false">learn </slidetext><slidetext slideid="63xAJGAN6uI.5dslNzzdaly" slidebank="false">let’s begin by exploring considerations for selecting a text or set of texts for a close reading. we will focus on the components of length and cognitive demand to determine appropriate texts.  first, reread the section “selecting short, worthy passages” from the article, “building capacity through close reading,” that you read to prepare for this session. next, in your participant workbook, reflect on why the length of the passage matters. also, reflect on what makes a passage worthy of close reading. finally, listen to miss johansen talk about her  strategy for choosing texts for close reading.   </slidetext><slidetext slideid="63xAJGAN6uI.6A4VR0EaoB8" slidebank="false">let’s begin by exploring considerations for selecting a text or set of texts for a close reading. we will focus on the components of length and cognitive demand to determine appropriate texts.  first, reread the section “selecting short, worthy passages” from the article, “building vocabulary through close reading” that you read to prepare for this session. next, in your participant workbook, reflect on why the length of the passage matters. also, reflect on what makes a passage worthy of close reading. finally, listen to miss johansen talk about her  strategy for choosing texts for close reading.   </slidetext><slidetext slideid="63xAJGAN6uI.6DqbsMDzbkd" slidebank="false">another important component to think about when implementing close reading into instruction is how to structure the questions that you ask during a close reading.  in the last session, you learned about text-dependent questions. the article you read also touched on this. teachers can scaffold instruction by guiding analysis with text-dependent questions.  listen to miss johansen talk about the way  she structures her questions to guide a  close reading.     </slidetext><slidetext slideid="63xAJGAN6uI.5wPbLZyylde" slidebank="false">another important component to think about when implementing close reading into instruction is how to structure the questions that you ask during a close reading.  in the last session, you learned about text-dependent questions. the article you read also touched on this. teachers can scaffold instruction by guiding analysis with text-dependent questions.  listen to miss johansen talk about the way  she structures her questions to guide a  close reading.     </slidetext><slidetext slideid="63xAJGAN6uI.64IY13jT1P6" slidebank="false">we know that historians read texts differently than scientists, and they both read differently than mathematicians. similarly, close readings across each discipline will be a slightly different experience for students.  we will compare two texts to examine the unique approach each discipline engages in during a close reading.  peruse the two texts and then reflect on the varying demands in your participant workbook.  science social studies after you have reflected on the varying demands of each discipline, click here to view  some sample responses.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="63xAJGAN6uI.6nXtKrbCg5T" slidebank="false">time for a quick-check!  respond to the close reading quick-check questions in your participant workbook. what is the purpose of a close reading?  how does a close reading help students build their vocabulary?  how might you structure your questions as you guide students through a close reading? </slidetext><slidetext slideid="63xAJGAN6uI.5uxz67BTFb9" slidebank="false">we will now examine one last component to think about when implementing close reading into instruction. as you guide students through a close reading, you will stop often to have students reread and reflect. careful consideration should be given to how you structure reflection.  the article that you read prior to this session suggests that students annotate the text while  they read. students mark text that they find confusing or thought-provoking.  listen to miss johansen share her  strategies for structuring reflection of  a close reading.    </slidetext><slidetext slideid="63xAJGAN6uI.67MNSINP4mW" slidebank="false">we will now examine one last component to think about when implementing close reading into instruction. as you guide students through a close reading, you will stop often to have students reread and reflect. careful consideration should be given to how you structure reflection.  the article that you read prior to this session suggests that students annotate the text while  they read. students mark text that they find confusing or thought-provoking.  listen to miss johansen share her  strategies for structuring reflection of  a close reading.    </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6VnXrL2qblR.6fW2JUgwvPo" slidebank="false">in practice </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6VnXrL2qblR.5yr2QAOZyRy" slidebank="false">let’s watch a close reading in action!  you will view david coleman, contributing author of the common core state standards, model a close reading of the text: “letter from birmingham jail” by martin luther king jr. click the video to begin.  click to download the text and  follow along during the close reading.  download text  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6VnXrL2qblR.6WteRdaFnjY" slidebank="false">reflect on the video in the participant workbook by responding to the following questions:  how was challenging vocabulary approached during the close reading? what kind of questions were asked and explored during the close reading? </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5kUV0fkYWnz.6O52XnadHjJ" slidebank="false">apply </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5kUV0fkYWnz.5x4JRVlvB2W" slidebank="false">mr. leonard teaches eighth-grade english language arts. he teaches a unit that focuses on the following essential questions:  what are the benefits and consequences of challenging social order?  what responsibilities do individuals have to respond to issues of social justice? during the unit, students read a variety of texts, including fiction and nonfiction. mr. leonard is preparing for a close reading of the famous “i have a dream” speech by martin luther king jr.   </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5kUV0fkYWnz.5V2XzZJvxnx" slidebank="false">mr. leonard:  i chose the “i have a dream” speech for a close reading for a few reasons. most students have heard excerpts from this speech before and have a general understanding of its message. however, it is a very complex text that brings up  many issues that students are usually unaware of.  students are usually most familiar with the ending of the speech, so i actually focus on the first half of the speech during the close reading. in the section that i focus on, there is some particularly challenging vocabulary and some contextualization is needed for students to understand the significance of king’s words.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5kUV0fkYWnz.6SHGa5OX1b1" slidebank="false">now it’s your turn!  select a complex text that you would like to use during instruction. decide whether you will do a close reading with the entire piece or a section of the piece that is worthy of a close reading.   use the close reading lesson organizer in the participant workbook to reflect on your rationale for your text selection.   </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5kUV0fkYWnz.6WlQtR2c3L4" slidebank="false">mr. leonard:  when i think about what skill i want to model for students in this close reading lesson, what sticks out is the challenging vocabulary in the speech. i know that students will become frustrated with the text as they encounter so many unfamiliar words, so what i want to do is model patient reading and strategies for grappling with challenging vocabulary. to do this, i’m going to focus on making my thinking transparent by thinking aloud a great deal during the lesson.  i will model analysis of the second paragraph for students, which will set them up for a guided close reading of the third paragraph. it may take me almost a full class period to model, but it will be worth the time spent.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5kUV0fkYWnz.5blTuXQM4S5" slidebank="false">now it’s your turn!  think about what skill you want to model for students in the close reading lesson. consider what skills students have little or no practice with.  use the close reading lesson organizer in the participant workbook to reflect on what you plan to model in the close reading lesson.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5kUV0fkYWnz.5iG741UfLSp" slidebank="false">mr. leonard:  after i have modeled a close reading of the second paragraph of the speech, i will engage in guided practice of close reading with students.  i will start by asking students to read the third paragraph silently. after they have read it once, i’ll read it aloud to students.  i always do this because it creates greater access to  listen to a fluent reader read the text. to get students engaged with the text, i will ask them  a series of text-dependent questions that get at the figurative language used, as well as trace the  definitions of two key phrases.   </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5kUV0fkYWnz.5wkWeO3Iebh" slidebank="false">mr. leonard:  one of the words that we will focus on is manacles. the word pinnacle came up in a story we just finished, so i want to see if students can apply their knowledge of suffixes and the study of the word pinnacle to help them determine the meaning of manacles.  i will do this close reading mostly in a whole group, but  as students are focusing on the significance of key  words and phrases, i’m going to have them do some  writing and partner sharing before we discuss in the  whole-group setting.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5kUV0fkYWnz.6I3j3oT1Uqn" slidebank="false">now it’s your turn!  think about how you will provide guided practice in the close reading lesson. consider what questions you might ask, how you will facilitate reflection, and what format you will use to discuss the text.   use the close reading lesson organizer in the participant workbook to reflect on your guided instruction in the close reading lesson.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5kUV0fkYWnz.6C9ra2TDnGr" slidebank="false">mr. leonard:  i have a few goals for students with this close reading lesson. first, i want them to persevere through the close reading and not give up with all the challenging vocabulary. second, once they have developed a deeper meaning of the significance of this speech, i want them to think about this text as evidence to use as they think about the essential questions from the unit.  i will ask students to respond to one of the essential  questions, using “i have a dream” as textual evidence. i will use their responses as formative assessment to evaluate how developed their close reading  skills are. </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5kUV0fkYWnz.6NOQMVWYLJP" slidebank="false">mr. leonard:  after i evaluate students, i plan on doing some close reading support by working with targeted small groups of students to do more modeling of close reading.  my long-term goal for students is for them to be able to closely read complex texts without my modeling or guidance.  this lesson is one small step toward that goal.   </slidetext><slidetext slideid="5kUV0fkYWnz.5qOjeKzzJMK" slidebank="false">now it’s your turn!  think about what you want students to be able to do independently during and after this lesson. you may want to look at state or district learning standards to help guide your thinking.   use the close reading lesson organizer in the participant workbook to reflect on what you want students to be able to do independently during and after the close reading lesson.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6bMBZx0L5ld.6anlHbEJ1hA" slidebank="false">reflect </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6bMBZx0L5ld.5cN1yA07wjF" slidebank="false">in the participant workbook, reflect on how the practice of close reading helps students develop vocabulary.   record any questions that you still have in the still wondering section of your participant workbook. you will have a chance to ask these questions to the facilitator in the live session later on in the course. ? ? ? </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6bMBZx0L5ld.5XHj5UeHUpW" slidebank="false">by the end of this blended course, you  will be able to do the following: understand vocabulary development and  spelling-meaning connections. analyze vocabulary assessments to identify  students’ needs and organize for instruction.  determine how best to instruct students using generative and word-specific strategies. select appropriate instructional strategies  and activities to expand students’ academic vocabulary.  today, we focused primarily on this outcome.  </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6QuSGxA16uD.65SEJjbLsuw" slidebank="false">look forward </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6QuSGxA16uD.6UjQg7tzjGu" slidebank="false">to apply what you have learned and engage in a professional discussion, respond to the discussion questions on the discussion board. remember to respond to at least two participants’ posts in addition to posting your own response to each question. </slidetext><slidetext slideid="6QuSGxA16uD.5tboUVrPLri" slidebank="false">in preparation for the next session, do the following: read chapter 7 of vocabulary their way. bring a copy of a vocabulary lesson plan and materials that you have taught in the past.  </slidetext></search></nav_data><resource_data description="&lt;p align='left'&gt;&lt;font face='Arial' size='11' color='#444444'&gt;Here are some useful links and documents:&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;" /><transcript_data /><glossary_data /></bwFrame>